            "text": "{\"study_topic_id\":\"study-b0e153b79ad6dd17580c\",\"overview\":{\"text\":\"Temperature inversion is an atmospheric layer in which air temperature rises with altitude instead of following the troposphere’s usual decrease. In the lower atmosphere this stable arrangement resists vertical mixing: cooler air can remain beneath warmer air, limiting convection and the upward spread of smoke and other pollutants. Such pollutants may build up near the ground and reduce visibility, although an inversion is a temperature pattern, not pollution itself. Near-surface inversions can form as ground cools on clear, light-wind nights; sinking air and fronts can produce other types. The stratosphere also has a temperature inversion because ozone absorbs ultraviolet radiation.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://www.weather.gov/jkl/education\",\"https://scied.ucar.edu/image/temperature-inversion\",\"https://www.weather.gov/media/zhu/ZHU_Training_Page/clouds/stability_clouds/stability_clouds2.pdf\"]},\"key_facts\":[{\"text\":\"In the troposphere, temperature usually decreases with altitude because the ground warms the air above it.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://www.weather.gov/jkl/education\"]},{\"text\":\"An inversion layer is stable and generally limits vertical cloud growth; in the lower atmosphere, it can also trap pollutants and reduce visibility.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://www.weather.gov/media/zhu/ZHU_Training_Page/clouds/stability_clouds/stability_clouds2.pdf\",\"https://scied.ucar.edu/image/temperature-inversion\"]},{\"text\":\"Common lower-atmosphere causes include surface cooling, sinking air associated with high pressure, and fronts between air masses.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://www.weather.gov/media/zhu/ZHU_Training_Page/clouds/stability_clouds/stability_clouds2.pdf\"]},{\"text\":\"Surface radiation inversions often form on clear, light-wind nights and are strongest near sunrise.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://www.weather.gov/media/zhu/ZHU_Training_Page/clouds/stability_clouds/stability_clouds2.pdf\"]},{\"text\":\"In the stratosphere, ozone absorbs ultraviolet radiation, warming the air with increasing altitude.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://www.weather.gov/jkl/education\"]}],\"sources\":[{\"title\":\"Weather Education\",\"url\":\"https://www.weather.gov/jkl/education\",\"publisher\":\"National Weather Service\",\"retrieved_at\":\"2026-09-24T18:50:34.968966Z\",\"supports\":\"Explains the usual temperature decrease with altitude in the troposphere and the temperature increase in the stratosphere due to ozone absorbing ultraviolet radiation.\"},{\"title\":\"Temperature Inversion\",\"url\":\"https://scied.ucar.edu/image/temperature-inversion\",\"publisher\":\"UCAR Center for Science Education\",\"retrieved_at\":\"2026-09-24T18:50:34.968966Z\",\"supports\":\"Illustrates how an inversion can inhibit rising air and trap near-surface pollution.\"},{\"title\":\"Temperature inversions (Fundamentals of Aviation Meteorology course notes)\",\"url\":\"https://www.weather.gov/media/zhu/ZHU_Training_Page/clouds/stability_clouds/stability_clouds2.pdf\",\"publisher\":\"Bureau of Meteorology Training Centre\",\"retrieved_at\":\"2026-09-24T18:50:34.968966Z\",\"supports\":\"Defines inversions, describes their stability and effects, and identifies surface radiation, subsidence, and frontal causes; explains when surface radiation inversions form.\"}],\"research_queries\":[\"site:weather.gov temperature inversion atmosphere education\",\"site:ucar.edu temperature inversion atmosphere explanation\",\"site:epa.gov temperature inversion air pollution\"],\"notes\":\"Overview is 100 words. Claims and key facts are linked to directly opened source pages; descriptions of near-ground pollutant trapping are limited to cases where an inversion inhibits mixing.\",\"unresolved_concerns\":[],\"self_check_complete\":true}"
          }
