    "overview": {
      "text": "Temperature inversion is an atmospheric layer in which air temperature rises with altitude instead of following the troposphere’s usual decrease. In the lower atmosphere this stable arrangement resists vertical mixing: cooler air can remain beneath warmer air, limiting convection and the upward spread of smoke and other pollutants. Such pollutants may build up near the ground and reduce visibility, although an inversion is a temperature pattern, not pollution itself. Near-surface inversions can form as ground cools on clear, light-wind nights; sinking air and fronts can produce other types. The stratosphere also has a temperature inversion because ozone absorbs ultraviolet radiation.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.weather.gov/jkl/education",
        "https://scied.ucar.edu/image/temperature-inversion",
        "https://www.weather.gov/media/zhu/ZHU_Training_Page/clouds/stability_clouds/stability_clouds2.pdf"
      ]
    },
    "key_facts": [
      {
        "text": "In the troposphere, temperature usually decreases with altitude because the ground warms the air above it.",
        "source_urls": [
          "https://www.weather.gov/jkl/education"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "An inversion layer is stable and generally limits vertical cloud growth; in the lower atmosphere, it can also trap pollutants and reduce visibility.",
        "source_urls": [
          "https://www.weather.gov/media/zhu/ZHU_Training_Page/clouds/stability_clouds/stability_clouds2.pdf",
          "https://scied.ucar.edu/image/temperature-inversion"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "Common lower-atmosphere causes include surface cooling, sinking air associated with high pressure, and fronts between air masses.",
        "source_urls": [
          "https://www.weather.gov/media/zhu/ZHU_Training_Page/clouds/stability_clouds/stability_clouds2.pdf"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "Surface radiation inversions often form on clear, light-wind nights and are strongest near sunrise.",
        "source_urls": [
          "https://www.weather.gov/media/zhu/ZHU_Training_Page/clouds/stability_clouds/stability_clouds2.pdf"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "In the stratosphere, ozone absorbs ultraviolet radiation, warming the air with increasing altitude.",
        "source_urls": [
          "https://www.weather.gov/jkl/education"
        ]
      }
    ],
